+| 1 — kernel | Draws the diffraction kernel itself into the **top-left corner** of the frame on a log tone curve (eight decades of intensity; anything linear is a black square with a white dot). The whole L x L texture is fitted into a square half the frame height. Shows what the FFT actually produced — ray count, the removed core, the halo an aberrated pupil adds — without hunting for a lamp bright enough. |
+| 2 — shape only | The sprite pass runs **exactly as it does normally** — same sources, same sprite size, same mip LOD — but the deposit ignores energy: every source that passed the threshold paints the pattern at full visibility on the same log curve mode 1 uses, coloured by the spectral sum renormalised to its own peak. Answers *does the pass draw at all, where does the pattern land, what is its scale on screen, what shape is it* — the questions a too-dim result cannot answer. Not a look: everything on screen is the pattern. |
+| 3 — sources | Draws the pass's own **thresholded source texture** (the excess above `HDR_Streaks_Threshold`) into the same corner, at half the frame height and in the frame's own aspect, on a four-decade grey scale: `v = log10(1 + E) / 4`, so E = 1 → 0.08, 10 → 0.26, 100 → 0.50, 1000 → 0.75, 10000 → 1.0. Once a second it also logs the brightest source on screen, over an asynchronous staging readback (issued one frame, mapped a few frames later — no stall): `[BLOOM] diffraction sources: max excess = 214.0 (threshold 12.0) at (238, 131) quarter-res`. If that number is small, no amount of pattern work will make rays appear. |
